Top 20 Docker Interview Questions
1. Explain Docker architecture.

Expected discussion:

Docker Client
Docker Daemon (dockerd)
Docker images
Containers
Docker Registry
Docker Engine
REST API

Interview flow:
docker CLI → Docker Daemon → Image → Container

2. What is the difference between a Docker image and a container?
Image	Container
Read-only template	Running instance of image
Used to create containers	Runs the application
Immutable	Has a writable layer
Can be stored in registry	Runs on Docker host

Example:

docker pull nginx
docker run -d nginx
3. What happens internally when you run docker run nginx?

You should be able to explain:

docker run nginx
       ↓
Docker CLI
       ↓
Docker daemon
       ↓
Check local image
       ↓
If not available → Pull from registry
       ↓
Create container
       ↓
Create network namespace
       ↓
Create writable container layer
       ↓
Start container process

This is a very common 5-year-level question.

4. Explain Dockerfile instructions.

Know these very well:

FROM
RUN
COPY
ADD
WORKDIR
ENV
ARG
EXPOSE
CMD
ENTRYPOINT
USER
VOLUME

Example:

FROM nginx:alpine

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/

EXPOSE 80

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
5. What is the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?

Example:

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

versus

ENTRYPOINT ["nginx"]
CMD ["-g", "daemon off;"]

A strong interview answer should explain:

Default command
Overriding behavior
Combining ENTRYPOINT + CMD
6. What is the difference between COPY and ADD?

COPY:

Copies files/directories into image
Simple and preferred in most cases

ADD:

Can copy files
Can extract local tar archives
Historically supports URL sources

Best practice: Prefer COPY unless you specifically need an ADD feature.

7. How do you reduce Docker image size?

Important 5-year question.

Mention:

Use Alpine/distroless images where appropriate
Multi-stage builds
.dockerignore
Don't install unnecessary packages
Combine appropriate RUN operations
Remove package caches
Use specific image tags
Don't copy unnecessary files
Keep build dependencies out of runtime image

Example:

FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build

WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src

RUN mvn package -DskipTests

FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre

COPY --from=build /app/target/app.jar /app/app.jar

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "/app/app.jar"]
8. What is a multi-stage Docker build?

It allows you to separate the build environment from the runtime environment.

Builder Image
   ↓
Compile application
   ↓
Generate artifact
   ↓
Runtime Image
   ↓
Copy only artifact

Benefits:

Smaller image
Better security
Faster deployment
No build tools in production
9. Explain Docker networking.

Know these networks:

docker network ls

Common network types:

bridge
host
none
overlay
macvlan

Example:

docker network create mynetwork

docker run -d --name app --network mynetwork nginx

Containers attached to the same Docker network can communicate using container/service names.

10. How does container-to-container communication work?

Suppose:

frontend
   ↓
backend
   ↓
mysql

Create network:

docker network create app-net

Run:

docker run -d --name mysql --network app-net mysql
docker run -d --name backend --network app-net backend-image

Backend can connect using:

mysql:3306

rather than relying on the container IP.

11. Explain Docker volumes. Why are they required?

Containers are ephemeral.

If you remove the container:

docker rm container

data in the container's writable layer can be lost.

Use volumes:

docker volume create mydata

docker run -d \
  -v mydata:/var/lib/mysql \
  mysql

Types to know:

Named volumes
Bind mounts
tmpfs mounts
12. What happens if a Docker container stops?

By default, the container stops, but the container itself still exists.

Check:

docker ps -a

Restart:

docker start container_name

For automatic restart:

docker run -d --restart=always nginx

Know restart policies:

no
always
on-failure
unless-stopped
13. A Docker container is continuously restarting. How will you troubleshoot?

This is a very important scenario question.

Start with:

docker ps

Then:

docker logs container_name

Inspect:

docker inspect container_name

Check the process:

docker top container_name

Check resources:

docker stats

Also verify:

Application error
Wrong CMD/ENTRYPOINT
Missing environment variables
Missing configuration
Port configuration
Permission problems
OOMKilled
Health check failures
Dependency/database connectivity
14. Docker container is running, but application is not accessible. What will you check?

Follow a systematic approach:

docker ps
docker logs <container>
docker inspect <container>
docker port <container>

Then check:

curl localhost:<port>

Check port mapping:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx

Meaning:

Host 8080 → Container 80

Then:

curl http://localhost:8080

Also check firewall/security-group/load-balancer rules when applicable.

15. What is the difference between EXPOSE and -p?

This is commonly asked.

EXPOSE 80

does not publish the port to the host.

It documents the port the application listens on.

Actual publishing:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx

means:

Host :8080
     ↓
Container :80
16. How do you troubleshoot a Docker container consuming high CPU or memory?

Use:

docker stats

Example:

CONTAINER     CPU %     MEM USAGE
app           95%       1.8GiB

Then:

docker top app
docker logs app
docker inspect app

Check:

Application process
Memory leak
Infinite loop
Traffic increase
Resource limits
OOM events

You can set limits:

docker run -d \
  --memory=512m \
  --cpus=1 \
  myapp
17. What are Docker layers?

Each applicable Dockerfile instruction creates an image layer.

Example:

FROM ubuntu
RUN apt-get update
RUN apt-get install nginx
COPY app /app

Conceptually:

Application layer
      ↓
COPY layer
      ↓
nginx installation layer
      ↓
apt layer
      ↓
Ubuntu base layer

Layers enable:

Caching
Reuse
Faster builds
Smaller storage requirements
18. How does Docker image caching work?

Suppose:

FROM node:20
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install
COPY . .
RUN npm build

If application source changes, Docker can reuse the earlier layers:

FROM             → CACHE
COPY package     → CACHE
npm install      → CACHE
COPY source      → changed
npm build        → rerun

This is why Dockerfile instruction ordering matters.

19. How do you secure Docker containers in production?

For a 5-year interview, mention several areas:

Use trusted/minimal base images
Scan images using tools such as Trivy
Don't run applications as root
Don't store secrets in Dockerfile
Use secrets management
Pin/verify image versions where appropriate
Regularly patch base images
Remove unnecessary packages
Use read-only filesystems where possible
Apply CPU/memory limits
Restrict container capabilities
Scan images in CI/CD

Example:

FROM nginx:alpine

RUN adduser -D appuser
USER appuser
20. Explain how Docker is used in a CI/CD pipeline.

This is extremely important for a 5-year DevOps interview.

Typical flow:

Developer
   ↓
Git
   ↓
Jenkins / GitHub Actions
   ↓
Build Application
   ↓
Run Unit Tests
   ↓
SonarQube
   ↓
Docker Build
   ↓
Trivy Security Scan
   ↓
Docker Image
   ↓
Push to Registry
   ↓
Deploy
   ↓
Kubernetes / ECS / VM

Example:

docker build -t myapp:1.0 .
docker tag myapp:1.0 registry/myapp:1.0
docker push registry/myapp:1.0

Then deployment uses:

registry/myapp:1.0
